  "programming": {
    "id": "creature-capture",
    "definition": {
      "title": "Creature Capture",
      "statement": "## Standard input and output\n\nEach run handles **one** case. Read from standard input and print the result to standard output. Do not read a case count or print case numbers, prompts, or labels. Use the line structure shown in the test-case input below.\n\n### Problem\n\nA creature is hiding in a cell of a 10x10 grid.\n\n{{diagram:p-15a02b1275b46b1e}}\n\nYou will be given the location of a guess and how far away the creature is from the guess. Find all possible cells where the creature could be located.\n\nEach cell in the grid is represented by a non-negative integer less than 100. The tens digit signifies the row (0-9, from top to bottom), and the units digit indicates the column (0-9, from left to right). An integer less than 10 indicates row 0.\n\nHow far the guess is from the creature’s location is called the distance value. It is a non-negative integer less than 100, where each digit indicates how far away the creature is horizontally or vertically from the guess. A value less than 10 means that one direction is 0.\n\nFor example, a distance value of 35 means that the creature could be 3 rows away and 5 columns away, or 5 rows away and 3 columns away. A distance value of 53 means the same possible locations of the creature as 35 does. The diagram illustrates a guess of 45 and a distance value of 35. The shaded cells with a question mark show the possible locations of the creature.\n\nAfter finding all possible cells in the grid where the creature could be hiding, output a string containing the smallest cell number and the largest cell number, in that order, separated by a single space.\n\n### Example\n\nInput:\n\n```text\n45\n35\n```\n\nOutput:\n\n```text\n10 98\n```\n\nExplanation The location of the guess is 45 and the distance value is 35. Using 3 as vertical and 5 as horizontal, the valid locations are 70 and 10. Using 5 as vertical and 3 as horizontal, the valid locations are 98 and 92. The smallest is 10 and the largest is 98, so output 10 98, in that order.\n\n### Input\n\nInput is two integers, both non-negative integers less than 100. The first is the guessed location. The second is the distance value, indicating how far the creature might be from the guessed location either horizontally or vertically.\n\n### Output\n\nOutput the smallest location where the creature could be hiding and the largest location as a string of these two integers (no leading 0s), separated by a single space.\n",
      "mode": "stdio",
      "comparator": "tokens",
      "timeLimitMs": 2000,
      "memoryLimitMb": 256,
      "starterCode": "# Read one case from standard input.\n# Print the result to standard output.\n",
